    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3661839/45444?iso=no": {
      "title": "Handelshøyskolen BI Digital",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Digital - Security Architect Handelshøyskolen BI søker Security Architect! Security Architect Søk her Vil du bidra til arbeidet med å forme og sikre de sentrale tjenestene som forbedrer den digitale hverdagen for våre søkere, studenter og ansatte? I avdelingen Digital på BI er vi ca. 80 ansatte som jobber tverrfaglig for å skape smidige og trygge brukeropplevelser. Du vil inngå i et nyopprettet team, Security and Digital Workplace , som har et helhetlig ansvar for både våre tekniske arbeidsflater og sikkerhet. Utdanningssektoren opplever et intenst digitalt trykk, og vi må ha en reell forsvarsevne for å beskytte verdifulle data mot stadig mer avanserte trusler. Nå ser vi etter en strategisk og operativ bygger som vil ha det overordnede blikket på sikkerheten, fra identitets- og klientmiljøet vårt til skyen. Om rollen Som Security Architect blir du CISOs operative og tekniske høyre hånd. Vi er i startgropen på vår skysikkerhetsreise i Azure, og du får muligheten til å forme sikkerhetslagene i tett samarbeid med våre flinke skyarkitekter og tech leads. Mens CISO eier det overordnede risikobildet, får du friheten og ansvaret til å bygge og eie arkitekturen. Rollen og ansvaret ditt vil være tydelig todelt: Du vil sikre våre tekniske arbeidsflater og identiteter (klient- og sluttbrukermiljø), samtidig som du bidrar til sikkerhetsarkitekturen og -forståelsen for utviklerne/arkitektene våre. Din viktigste oppgave blir å balansere dette overordnede ansvaret, slik at viktige data ikke kommer på avveie. Trives du med å omsette sikkerhetsteori til operative tiltak og bygge fremtidsrettede løsninger fra bunnen av, vil du passe perfekt inn. Her er veien fra idé til utførelse kort, og du vil samarbeide tett med dyktige systemeiere, arkitekter og utviklere. Dine viktigste ansvarsområder: Sikkerhetsarkitektur: Eie og drive det tekniske veikartet for sikkerhet fra dagens situasjon til målbildet i tett samarbeid med CISO. Sky og Infrastruktur: Drive trusselmodellering (f.eks. STRIDE), definere sikkerhetskrav for våre utviklingsløp (DevSecOps) i samarbeid med skyarkitektene, og bygge opp skysikkerheten (CSPM) og overvåkingen fra bunnen av. Dette inkluderer også design og etablering av vår egen sikkerhetsoperasjon (SOC). Identitet og Klient: Sette standarder og arkitekturprinsipper for Zero Trust, tilgangsstyring og endepunktssikkerhet, i tett samarbeid med Platform-teamet. KI-sikkerhet: Utnytte kunstig intelligens som et offensivt sikkerhetsverktøy, og samtidig sikre organisasjonens innføring av KI mot nye angrepsmetoder (LLM-trusler, uautorisert bruk av KI). Etterlevelse og risiko: Operasjonalisere rammeverkene og understøtte CISOs ansvar for etterlevelse av GDPR, NIS2 og NSMs grunnprinsipper. Sektorsamarbeid: Jobbe strategisk mot Sikt og eduCSC for felles trusselovervåking i utdanningssektoren. Teknisk miljø og metodikk Sikkerhetsporteføljen vår er tett knyttet til Microsoft Azure. Vi vil ikke bare at du skal drifte det vi har, men at du tenker nytt og automatiserer der du kan. Teknologiene og verktøyene du vil få eierskap til inkluderer: Sikkerhetsplattform: Dybdebruk av Microsofts sikkerhetsportefølje, med hovedvekt på Sentinel, Defender-suiten og Entra ID. Deteksjon og trusseljakting: Utvikling av komplekse deteksjonsregler med KQL (Kusto Query Language). Automasjon (SOAR): Skripte og bygge automatiserte responsrutiner ved hjelp av Logic Apps, PowerShell og Python. Infrastruktur som Kode (IaC): Integrere sikkerhet direkte i infrastrukturen ved bruk av Bicep eller Terraform. KI-verktøy: Praktisk bruk av verktøy som Microsoft Copilot for Security for hendelseshåndtering og analyse. Hvem er du? Vi ser etter en sjelden kombinasjon: Du evner å tenke langsiktig strategi, samtidig som du trives med hendene på tastaturet. Du har minst 5 års relevant operativ og taktisk erfaring med sikkerhetsarkitektur. Du har en sterk teknisk grunnmur og evner å bygge bro mellom digital arbeidsplass (M365) og skyinfrastruktur (Azure IaaS/PaaS). Du evner å se sammenhengen mellom tekniske sårbarheter og vårt faktiske risikobilde. Du bidrar med faglig retning og fungerer som en sentral, støttende fagressurs for kollegene dine. Du har høyere IT-utdanning (relevant erfaring og sertifiseringer kan kompensere for manglende grad). Hva BI tilbyr Hos oss blir du en del av BI Digital, en avdeling med 80 medarbeidere i toppmoderne lokaler i Nydalen. En sentral rolle som sikrer løsninger med stor samfunnsmessig betydning. Konkurransedyktige betingelser og meget gode ordninger for pensjon og forsikring. Svært god balanse mellom jobb og fritid: Fleksibel arbeidstid, fri i romjula og påskeuka, i tillegg til fem ukers ferie. Stor grad av faglig utvikling, inkludert tilgang til BIs masterstudier, sertifiseringer og kurs. Arbeidssted vil være BIs campus i Oslo (Nydalen). Hvis du tenker at denne stillingen passer for deg, er du velkommen til å søke. Har du spørsmål, ta gjerne kontakt med leder Yngve Nordy, Yngve Nordy. Skriv ut Søk her Del på: Fylke: Oslo Jobbtype: Fast Heltid/Deltid: Heltid Arbeidstid: Dag Søknadsfrist: 27.09.2026 Tiltredelse: Etter avtale Arbeidssted: Oslo Kontaktpersoner: Yngve Nordby mob: +47 99555575 Hjemmeside: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Digital Handelshøyskolen BI er en internasjonal handelshøyskole i verdensklasse. Med over 20.000 studenter, 1000 ansatte og 300.000 alumni, er BI også regnet som en av de største og mest innflytelsesrike handelshøyskolene i Europa. BI tilbyr en variert og omfattende portefølje av bachelor-, master-, PhD-, og videreutdanningsprogrammer og kurs. Våre ulike partnerskap og samarbeid med ledende norske og internasjonale selskaper, organisasjoner og institusjoner bidrar til å heve kvaliteten på vårt utdanningstilbud. BIs Strategi 2030 har definert tydelige mål for å sikre at skolen leverer kunnskap som forandrer gjennom førsteklasses forskning og enestående læringsopplevelser. Vi gir virksomheter kunnskapen de trenger for å lykkes og mennesker muligheten til å bygge meningsfylte karrierer. Tilbake til toppen English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × Dette nettstedet bruker informasjonskapsler Vi bruker informasjonskapsler for å tilpasse innhold, annonser og analysere trafikken vår. Vi deler også informasjon om din bruk av nettstedet vårt med våre annonserings- og analysepartnere som kan kombinere den med annen informasjon du har gitt dem eller som de har samlet inn fra din bruk av tjenestene deres. Privacy Policy Vis detaljer Skjul detaljer Lagre og lukk Godta alle Avvis alle"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3621563/45439?iso=no": {
      "title": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for rettsvitenskap og styring",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for rettsvitenskap og styring - Department of Law and Governance - PhD Candidate in Administrative Sanctions PhD position in administrative and criminal law at BI Norwegian Business School, exploring rule of law and administrative sanctions within the RODEAS research project (2026–2030). PhD Candidate in Administrative Sanctions Send application Department of Law and Governance at BI Norwegian Business School announces a position as a PhD candidate in administrative law and criminal law within the project RODEAS: Rule of Law, Due Process, and Effectivity in Administrative Sanctioning. RODEAS is funded by the Research Council of Norway (2026–2030). The position is for four years with 25% teaching or other career-promoting duties. Project description The RODEAS project will analyse administrative sanctions broadly and interdisciplinarily. Core research topics include the relationship between administrative sanctions and punishment, general principles for imposing administrative sanctions, the need for a dedicated process for imposing sanctions, and proportionality as a guiding principle in sentencing. Using an established data research platform, the project will also conduct large-scale analyses of sanctions decisions by selected partner regulators. This empirical evidence will be used in the project’s various WPs, including the project which is now advertised. The overall ambition of the project is to contribute to the development of a normative framework within which the administrative sanctions regime can function in a way that meets the considerations of efficiency, legal certainty and predictability. The PhD project should be anchored in the project's WP 1, which focuses on the legal nature of the administrative sanctioning regime as situated in the intersection between criminal law punishment and purely restrictive measures (WP1 is described here, project description ). While most administrative sanctions are imposed as a reaction to a violation of a legal norm, and are justified by deterrence, they are often not intended to convey blame in the same sense as criminal punishment. The justification for administrative sanctions is blurred and clear and coherent principles guiding the functional relationship between administrative sanctions and criminal penalties are lacking. The candidate’s project should analyse the legal nature of the administrative sanctioning regime as situated between criminal law punishment and administrative sanctions, how it can be theoretically justified and how such justifications align with current legislative and administrative practices. While the candidate will design and pursue his/her own research project anchored in WP 1, the final project description will be developed in consultation with the supervisor, the project manager and RODEAS’s research team following the appointment. Expectations and commitments The Department of Law and Governance is responsible for BI’s master’s program in Law. In addition to the doctoral work, the candidate will be expected to deliver teaching of high academic and pedagogical quality to an extent that corresponds to the teaching duties for the position. The Department of Law and Governance focuses on quality and competence development. Good pedagogical activities support the process for further academic career development. It is an ambition that BI, through its academic work, distinguishes itself on the international research arena, and develops the students' academic competence and skills through high quality teaching. Relevance and influence on practice is ensured through close collaboration with the working world. The teaching must build on the latest research, be practically relevant, and further the development of academic skills amongst the students. The candidate is expected to join and actively participate in the research community at the Department of Law and Governance at BI Norwegian Business school, Campus Oslo, and to be based there. The doctoral candidate may be expected to be responsible for organising various joint activities within the RODEAS project. The candidate is also expected to participate in the RODEAS’s activities. Qualification requirements • A Norwegian five-year Master’s degree in Law or an equivalent qualification • The PhD project should preferably be conducted in English, but applications in Norwegian are also welcomed. Applicants must demonstrate excellent academic proficiency in either English or Norwegian, both written and spoken. • The candidate's project must correspond to the purpose and objectives of the RODEAS project • As BI is not accredited for awarding PhDs in law, the PhD project will be affiliated with either the University of Oslo or the University of Bergen. The candidate must therefore qualify for the PhD programme at one of these institutions Generelt om opptak på ph.d - programmet ved UiO Veiledning for søkere til ph.d.  -stillinger ved UiB Other assessment criteria • The quality of the academic project description (5-10 pages) • Previous experience and academic publications, particularly in criminal law and/or administrative law • The candidate’s ability to work goal-oriented, systematically, creatively, and independently • We are looking for a candidate with good communication skills • We are looking for a candidate with a collaborative, friendly, and team-oriented style of work, and an ability to join interdisciplinary and international academic communities What we can offer Exciting and meaningful tasks in an organization with an important societal mission, contributing to knowledge development and knowledge-based education A very good working environment at one of the leading business schools in the Nordic region Supportive and agreeable academic and research environment Strong focus on professional career development Membership in BI's collective pension scheme Good welfare schemes Salary NOK 632.200 Application Process Candidates interested in the position must submit their application electronically via the \"Send Application\" button. The following documents must be uploaded: • Application Letter • Academic project description (5-10 pages) • CV and publication list • Diploma from the highest degree obtained • Other documentation that may be of importance for the assessment of the applicant’s competence level The application deadline is 15th November, 2026. No materials can be submitted after the deadline Qualifications will be assessed according to the usual guidelines for academic positions at universities and specialized university institutions, as well as BI's employment guidelines. In assessing applicants, priority will be given to the project description, their academic achievements, previous relevant publications, and their professional and personal qualifications, especially regarding their ability to complete the proposed project within the specified timeframe. BI Norwegian Business School emphasizes equality and diversity. Qualified applicants can be called in for an interview. BI Norwegian Business School reserves the right not to appoint qualified applicants if the candidate is considered not to satisfy the requirement for personal collaboration or other specific requirements for the position. More information For more information about the position, please contact the project manager Professor Johan Boucht, telephone + 47 47155 5403, e-mail: johan.boucht@bi.no . For more information about the department and BI Norwegian Business School, visit Department of Law and Governance, BI Print Send application Share on: Region: Oslo Job type: Fixed Term Contract Working hours: Full-time Working days: Day Application deadline: 15/11/2026 Expected start date: Fall 2026 Location: Campus Oslo Contacts: Johan Boucht Telephone: +47 47155403 Company homepage: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for rettsvitenskap og styring - Department of Law and Governance BI Norwegian Business School is ranked as Norway’s #1 business school and one of the best in Europe. With more than 20.000 students, 1000 employees and 300.000 alumni, it is also one of the largest and most influential business schools in Europe. BI is considered the main provider of business education in Norway, offering a diverse and comprehensive portfolio of bachelor’s, master’s, PhD and executive study programmes and courses. Our different partnerships and collaborations with leading Norwegian and international companies, organisations and institutions further enhance the quality of our study programmes. Towards 2030, BI aims to deliver knowledge that truly makes a difference through world-class research and outstanding learning experiences, giving businesses the knowledge to succeed and people the opportunity to build meaningful careers. Back to the top English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × This website uses cookies We use cookies to personalise content, ads and to analyse our traffic. We also share information about your use of our site with our advertising and analytics partners who may combine it with other information that you’ve provided to them or that they’ve collected from your use of their services. Privacy Policy Show details Hide details Save and close Accept all Decline all"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3663827/45435?iso=no": {
      "title": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur - Department of Communication and Culture  - PhD specialisation in Communication, EMANAIRE project: Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI” PhD specialisation in Communication, EMANAIRE project: Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI Søk her BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Futureproofing Employee Careers in the Age of Generative AI”. About EMANAIRE EMANAIRE is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. Its projects examine what happens when generative and agentic AI enters leadership, everyday workflows, team communication, professional learning and career decisions. Across the network, human agency means the practical ability to understand AI-supported decisions, exercise judgement, question outputs and shape how work is organised. Each researcher joins an international cohort, receives supervision across institutions and takes part in three residential schools, methods workshops, monthly research seminars and joint work with organisations beyond academia. Every project includes a planned six-month intersectoral secondment. The doctoral project Two employees can encounter the same generative AI roll-out and see very different futures. One may find new ways to use their skills; another may fear redundancy, lose confidence or see no credible path to reskilling. Generative AI is changing what many jobs consist of and which skills are valued in them, and the consequences for individual careers are uneven and hard to predict. Employees face open questions about which parts of their work will remain theirs and whether they can realistically acquire the skills that will matter next. Organisations, in turn, need to understand why the same technology is experienced as an opportunity by some employees and as a threat by others, and what they can do so that adjustment does not depend on individual luck or resources. This doctoral project studies how employees make sense of generative AI in relation to their own careers, and how the conditions they work under shape what follows from that assessment. It asks how career adaptability, supervisory and organisational support, opportunities to use and develop skills, and job satisfaction and engagement help explain why some employees come through AI-driven change with sustainable careers while others lose ground. Reskilling and internal mobility receive particular attention as routes to sustainable career outcomes, together with the forms of support and communication that make such routes credible to employees. The doctorate will produce evidence on the patterns of risk and resilience that emerge as generative AI enters workplaces, and on the organisational conditions that help employees retain agency over their own careers. Its academic contribution lies in explaining why similar technological changes lead to different career trajectories across employees and workplaces. It should also result in practical guidance that helps organisations recognise where employees feel confident, constrained or anxious and respond with appropriate support and communication during AI-driven change. The specific research questions and design are not fixed in advance and will be developed together with the supervisory team. Applicants are encouraged to bring their own ideas to the project proposal that forms part of the application. Your work Examine how employees appraise generative AI as a threat or an opportunity for their own careers, and how these appraisals relate to career adaptability, skill use, job satisfaction and engagement. Study how supervisory and organisational support shape risk and resilience during AI-driven change, and when reskilling and internal mobility lead to sustainable career outcomes. Identify the conditions under which employees retain agency over their careers as generative AI changes the content of their work. Translate the findings into guidance for organisations on supporting employees and communicating during AI-driven change. Refine the research questions and design with the supervision team and prepare ethics, data-management and open-science materials. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research outputs under the host doctoral programme and EMANAIRE plan. Take part in local and network-wide training, cohort activities, reviews, dissemination and practitioner engagement. Undertake the six-month intersectoral secondment and the separate academic mobility visit. Contribute to research integrity, equality, responsible AI, open science and FAIR data practices. Who we are looking for The candidate will join BI's four-year doctoral programme, designed for talented students who wish to pursue an international academic career and contribute to the international research community through high-quality publications. Recruitment of PhD candidates is an important part of the department's research agenda and BI's ambition to be a leading European business school. Eligible applicants must have completed, or complete by the start date, a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a research master's/M.Phil. degree, or a comparable degree relevant to the PhD specialisation in Communication. Relevant disciplinary backgrounds include, but are not limited to, communication, media studies, human-computer interaction, information systems, sociology, psychology, management, organisational behaviour, human resource management, law and technology, science and technology studies, political science, or related social-science and interdisciplinary fields. An MBA degree does not fulfil the requirements for admission. For this project, the following qualities will be viewed as advantageous: Interest in careers, HRM, organisational or work psychology, reskilling or generative AI at work. Strong quantitative or mixed-methods skills and interest in measurement and longitudinal organisational research. Experience with surveys, multivariate analysis, person-centred approaches or data visualisation would be advantageous. Ability to translate employee-level evidence into organisation-level insight and practical guidance. Strong academic writing skills and motivation to publish in international peer-reviewed journals. Supervision and integration at BI The selected candidate will receive close supervision and follow-up from research faculty. The main supervisors are Professor Christian Fieseler and Associate Professor Dominique Kost (BI Norwegian Business School). The candidate will be integrated into the Department of Communication and Culture and the wider EMANAIRE network. Employment and support The four-year scholarship is currently NOK 623,200 gross per year and will be adjusted according to applicable regulations. The position provides membership in the Norwegian Public Service Pension Fund (Statens Pensjonskasse). Twenty-five per cent of working time is reserved for teaching. The candidate's workplace will be BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo, Norway. Further information about the doctoral programme and admission requirements is available from BI's PhD Administration (phd@bi.no) and the PhD specialisation in Communication: https://www.bi.no/en/programmes-and-individual-courses/phd/communication/. How to apply Candidates are asked to upload the following material with their electronic application: Certificate of a A Master degree (MSc, MA, or equivalent) , including transcripts of grades. The qualifying degree must include thesis work and be completed by the start date, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not meet the admission requirements. Certificate of a bachelor's degree or equivalent, including transcripts of grades. BI requires a two-year master's programme and average grades of B or higher for eligibility. A Diploma Supplement, or a comparable document explaining the study and grading system and the rights for further study associated with the degree. Other relevant certificates and transcripts. A 2,000-word original project proposal that engages independently with this doctoral project, including the proposed research focus, methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Curriculum vitae. At least two letters of recommendation from relevant employers, professors or supervisors that provide evidence of the applicant's research potential. Certificates from previously completed doctoral-level courses, if requesting approval of these courses for the BI PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School. A complete list of publications and other documented relevant activities, where applicable. Signed verification document. Applicants who do not hold a qualifying master's degree from a Nordic institution and whose qualifying degree was not taught in English must normally document English proficiency through TOEFL or IELTS, unless a waiver is granted. The minimum accepted scores are: TOEFL: 575 on the written test, 233 on the computer-based test, or 90 on the internet-based test. IELTS: overall score of 6.5. TOEFL and IELTS scores must be from the preceding two years. The BI institution codes are GMAT 0529, TOEFL 0529 and GRE 7365. IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. Applicants who believe they qualify for a waiver should submit the required waiver documentation. Please note that all documents must be uploaded in the electronic application system. Documents may not be submitted by e-mail. Furthermore, documents submitted after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master’s degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that applicants will not be able to revise their application once it has been submitted. Candidates may later be asked for further information and/or invited to an interview. A candidate admitted to the programme must prepare a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. To be eligible for MSCA funding, the selected candidate must, on the first day of MSCA employment: (i) not hold a doctoral degree; candidates who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis but have not yet formally received the degree are not eligible; (ii) comply with the MSCA mobility rule, meaning they must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Norway for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding that date, excluding compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention; and (iii) be enrolled in, or meet the conditions required for timely enrolment in, the doctoral programme leading to the degree specified in this notice. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of the host institution's doctoral admission requirements. More information Questions about the project can be addressed to Professor Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication can be addressed to Academic Program Manager Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the application process can be sent to BI's PhD Administration at phd@bi.no. Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Skriv ut Søk her Del på: Fylke: Oslo Jobbtype: Åremål Heltid/Deltid: Heltid Arbeidstid: Dag Søknadsfrist: 01.11.2026 Tiltredelse: 01.01.2027 Arbeidssted: Oslo Kontaktpersoner: Christian Fieseler Hjemmeside: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur - Department of Communication and Culture Handelshøyskolen BI er en internasjonal handelshøyskole i verdensklasse. Med over 20.000 studenter, 1000 ansatte og 300.000 alumni, er BI også regnet som en av de største og mest innflytelsesrike handelshøyskolene i Europa. BI tilbyr en variert og omfattende portefølje av bachelor-, master-, PhD-, og videreutdanningsprogrammer og kurs. Våre ulike partnerskap og samarbeid med ledende norske og internasjonale selskaper, organisasjoner og institusjoner bidrar til å heve kvaliteten på vårt utdanningstilbud. BIs Strategi 2030 har definert tydelige mål for å sikre at skolen leverer kunnskap som forandrer gjennom førsteklasses forskning og enestående læringsopplevelser. Vi gir virksomheter kunnskapen de trenger for å lykkes og mennesker muligheten til å bygge meningsfylte karrierer. Tilbake til toppen English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × Dette nettstedet bruker informasjonskapsler Vi bruker informasjonskapsler for å tilpasse innhold, annonser og analysere trafikken vår. Vi deler også informasjon om din bruk av nettstedet vårt med våre annonserings- og analysepartnere som kan kombinere den med annen informasjon du har gitt dem eller som de har samlet inn fra din bruk av tjenestene deres. Privacy Policy Vis detaljer Skjul detaljer Lagre og lukk Godta alle Avvis alle"
    },
    "https://bi.easycruit.com/vacancy/3663821/45435?iso=no": {
      "title": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur",
      "description": "Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur - Department of Communication and Culture  - PhD specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams”. PhD specialisation in Communication - EMANAIRE project: Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams Søk her BI Norwegian Business School's Department of Communication and Culture invites applications for this four-year doctoral scholarship in the EMANAIRE project “Managing Team Communication in AI-Augmented Teams”. About EMANAIRE EMANAIRE is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network bringing together 15 doctoral candidates at nine European universities to study how AI is changing work. Its projects examine what happens when generative and agentic AI enters leadership, everyday workflows, team communication, professional learning and career decisions. Across the network, human agency means the practical ability to understand AI-supported decisions, exercise judgement, question outputs and shape how work is organised. Each researcher joins an international cohort, receives supervision across institutions and takes part in three residential schools, methods workshops, monthly research seminars and joint work with organisations beyond academia. Every project includes a planned six-month intersectoral secondment. The doctoral project When a team delegates drafting, analysis or coordination to AI, communication changes even if the organisational chart does not. Colleagues may exchange fewer early ideas, scrutinise outputs less closely or become uncertain about whose judgement shaped the final result. This doctoral project investigates how the outsourcing of essential tasks to AI changes the way leaders and teams communicate and collaborate. Particular attention will be paid to team dynamics, including behavioral aspects such as AI overreliance, disclosure of AI use, and patterns of AI-supported communication; cognitive aspects such as beliefs, assumptions, and mindsets regarding digital teamwork and AI-assisted collaboration; and managerial and personal resources that help employees adapt to changing work demands and maintain critical thinking. The project combines empirical research with scale development and validation to better understand the conditions under which AI-supported collaboration strengthens, rather than undermines, communication quality, team effectiveness, and critical reflection. The doctorate will produce evidence about the communication patterns that emerge in AI-augmented teams and the leadership practices that shape them. It should also deliver validated measures and manager-facing workshops or tools that help teams notice weak scrutiny, protect critical thinking and use delegation without hollowing out collective learning. Findings will be translated into practical tools, workshops, and evidence-based recommendations for managers. The candidate can tailor the research design to some extent: A programme combining measure development with field or team studies is plausible after discussion with the supervisory team about samples, settings, methods and analysis. Your work Examine how delegating work to AI affects leaders' work, team dynamics, AI overreliance and disclosure, implicit theories and mindsets related to digital teams, and critical scrutiny Develop and validate quantitative measures capturing team dynamics in AI assisted teams, and/or leader-employee communication in such settings Identify managerial and team-level resources that help employees meet changing demands from agentic and generative AI and translate the findings into manager-facing workshops and practices. Study whether over-delegation weakens critical scrutiny, skill development and developmental teamwork. Refine the research questions and design with the supervision team and prepare ethics, data-management and open-science materials. Complete a doctoral thesis and related research outputs under the host doctoral programme and EMANAIRE plan. Take part in local and network-wide training, cohort activities, reviews, dissemination and practitioner engagement. Undertake the six-month intersectoral secondment and the separate academic mobility visit. Contribute to research integrity, equality, responsible AI, open science and FAIR data practices. Who we are looking for The candidate will join BI's four-year doctoral programme, designed for talented students who wish to pursue an international academic career and contribute to the international research community through high-quality publications. Recruitment of PhD candidates is an important part of the department's research agenda and BI's ambition to be a leading European business school. Eligible applicants must have completed, or complete by the start date, a Master of Science degree with thesis work, a research master's/M.Phil. degree, or a comparable degree relevant to the PhD specialisation in Communication. Relevant disciplinary backgrounds include, but are not limited to, communication, media studies, human-computer interaction, information systems, sociology, psychology, management, organisational behaviour, human resource management, law and technology, science and technology studies, political science, or related social-science and interdisciplinary fields. An MBA degree does not fulfil the requirements for admission. For this project, the following qualities will be viewed as advantageous: Knowledge of, or a strong interest in, team dynamics, including behavioral aspects such as AI overreliance, disclosure of AI use, and patterns of AI-supported communication, and cognitive patterns regarding digital teamwork and AI-assisted collaboration Strong quantitative or mixed-methods skills, with interest in measurement development and validation. Experience with surveys, experiments, field studies, team research or psychometric analysis would be advantageous. Ability to connect individual, leader and team-level processes in a rigorous research design. Strong academic writing skills and motivation to publish in international peer-reviewed journals. Interest in connecting research and practice through intensive collaboration with the secondment partner Supervision and integration at BI The selected candidate will receive close supervision and follow-up from research faculty. The main supervisors are Professors Suzanne van Gils and Sut I Wong (BI Norwegian Business School). The candidate will be integrated into the Department of Communication and Culture and the wider EMANAIRE network. Employment and support The four-year scholarship is currently NOK 623,200 gross per year and will be adjusted according to applicable regulations. The position provides membership in the Norwegian Public Service Pension Fund (Statens Pensjonskasse). Twenty-five per cent of working time is reserved for teaching. The candidate's workplace will be BI Norwegian Business School in Oslo, Norway. Further information about the doctoral programme and admission requirements is available from BI's PhD Administration (phd@bi.no) and the PhD specialisation in Communication: https://www.bi.no/en/programmes-and-individual-courses/phd/communication/. How to apply Candidates are asked to upload the following material with their electronic application: Certificate of a A Master degree (MSc, MA, or equivalent) , including transcripts of grades. The qualifying degree must include thesis work and be completed by the start date, with a grade point average of B or higher. An MBA degree does not meet the admission requirements. Certificate of a bachelor's degree or equivalent, including transcripts of grades. BI requires a two-year master's programme and average grades of B or higher for eligibility. A Diploma Supplement, or a comparable document explaining the study and grading system and the rights for further study associated with the degree. Other relevant certificates and transcripts. A 2,000-word original project proposal that engages independently with this doctoral project, including the proposed research focus, methods, schedule and relevant literature. Any use of generative AI to produce text must be clearly stated and cited. Curriculum vitae. At least two letters of recommendation from relevant employers, professors or supervisors that provide evidence of the applicant's research potential. Certificates from previously completed doctoral-level courses, if requesting approval of these courses for the BI PhD programme at BI Norwegian Business School. A complete list of publications and other documented relevant activities, where applicable. Signed verification document. Applicants who do not hold a qualifying master's degree from a Nordic institution and whose qualifying degree was not taught in English must normally document English proficiency through TOEFL or IELTS, unless a waiver is granted. The minimum accepted scores are: TOEFL: 575 on the written test, 233 on the computer-based test, or 90 on the internet-based test. IELTS: overall score of 6.5. TOEFL and IELTS scores must be from the preceding two years. The BI institution codes are GMAT 0529, TOEFL 0529 and GRE 7365. IELTS copies are accepted when the TRF number is visible. Applicants who believe they qualify for a waiver should submit the required waiver documentation. Please note that all documents must be uploaded in the electronic application system. Documents may not be submitted by e-mail. Furthermore, documents submitted after the application deadline will not be considered. Exceptions will only be made for students who are completing their master’s degree in 2026 and do not have a final certificate when applying. Please note that applicants will not be able to revise their application once it has been submitted. Candidates may later be asked for further information and/or invited to an interview. A candidate admitted to the programme must prepare a more detailed progress plan with milestones and courses for the four years of study. MSCA eligibility Applicants may be of any nationality. To be eligible for MSCA funding, the selected candidate must, on the first day of MSCA employment: (i) not hold a doctoral degree; candidates who have successfully defended a doctoral thesis but have not yet formally received the degree are not eligible; (ii) comply with the MSCA mobility rule, meaning they must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Norway for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding that date, excluding compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent in a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention; and (iii) be enrolled in, or meet the conditions required for timely enrolment in, the doctoral programme leading to the degree specified in this notice. The appointment is conditional on documentary verification of these conditions and fulfilment of the host institution's doctoral admission requirements. More information Questions about the project can be addressed to Professor Suzanne van Gils at suzanne.v.gils@bi.no. Questions about the PhD specialisation in Communication can be addressed to Academic Program Manager  Christian Fieseler at christian.fieseler@bi.no. Questions about the application process can be sent to BI's PhD Administration at phd@bi.no. Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Skriv ut Søk her Del på: Fylke: Oslo Jobbtype: Åremål Heltid/Deltid: Heltid Arbeidstid: Dag Søknadsfrist: 01.11.2026 Tiltredelse: 01.01.2027 Arbeidssted: Oslo Kontaktpersoner: Christian Fieseler Hjemmeside: http://www.bi.no Handelshøyskolen BI Institutt for kommunikasjon og kultur - Department of Communication and Culture Handelshøyskolen BI er en internasjonal handelshøyskole i verdensklasse. Med over 20.000 studenter, 1000 ansatte og 300.000 alumni, er BI også regnet som en av de største og mest innflytelsesrike handelshøyskolene i Europa. BI tilbyr en variert og omfattende portefølje av bachelor-, master-, PhD-, og videreutdanningsprogrammer og kurs. Våre ulike partnerskap og samarbeid med ledende norske og internasjonale selskaper, organisasjoner og institusjoner bidrar til å heve kvaliteten på vårt utdanningstilbud. BIs Strategi 2030 har definert tydelige mål for å sikre at skolen leverer kunnskap som forandrer gjennom førsteklasses forskning og enestående læringsopplevelser. Vi gir virksomheter kunnskapen de trenger for å lykkes og mennesker muligheten til å bygge meningsfylte karrierer. Tilbake til toppen English Latvian Danish Czech Dutch Estonian German Spanish Swedish Finnish French Italian Lithuanian Norwegian Polish × Dette nettstedet bruker informasjonskapsler Vi bruker informasjonskapsler for å tilpasse innhold, annonser og analysere trafikken vår. Vi deler også informasjon om din bruk av nettstedet vårt med våre annonserings- og analysepartnere som kan kombinere den med annen informasjon du har gitt dem eller som de har samlet inn fra din bruk av tjenestene deres. Privacy Policy Vis detaljer Skjul detaljer Lagre og lukk Godta alle Avvis alle"
